          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.119,
            0.5,
            0.141
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "\\*Visualising Volume Formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.156,
            0.856,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "Here is a way to see the link between the formulas for volume of a cone, surface area of a sphere and volume of a sphere. Recall that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.198,
            0.76,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { V o l u m e ~ o f ~ c o n e } = { \\frac { 1 } { 3 } } \\; { \\mathrm { a r e a ~ o f ~ b a s e } } \\times { \\mathrm { h e i g h t ~ o f ~ c o n e } } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.234,
            0.858,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": "Imagine that the entire surface of the sphere is divided into tiny regions. The shapes of these regions do not matter; what matters is that they are very tiny. Now connect all these regions to the centre of the sphere. This creates a large number of thin cones. They all have the same height, namely r. The volume of the sphere is equal to the sum of the volumes of all the cones. Using the fact that the cones all have the same height, we may write:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.353,
            0.801,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "Volume of sphere $= { \\frac { r } { 3 } }$ × sum of base areas of all the thin cones"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.281,
            0.384,
            0.722,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": "$= { \\frac { r } { 3 } } \\times$ surface area of sphere $\\frac{r}{3} \\times 4\\pi r^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^{3}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.435,
            0.79,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": "",
